    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`). The full suite here consists of the acceptance tests, all green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented create-time address normalization following the project's existing normalize-and-compare patterns (mirroring how email and telephone are handled), keeping each responsibility in its own small unit.\n\n**New files**\n- `Addresses.java` \u2014 the single source of truth for address canonicalization: trims, collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands whole-word street-type abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). Null-safe, sits alongside the existing `Emails`/`Telephones` helpers.\n- `NormalizeOwnerAddress.java` \u2014 a pipeline step that writes the normalized address back onto the request body so later steps persist and return the canonical form (mirrors `NormalizeOwnerEmail`/`NormalizeOwnerTelephone`).\n\n**Changed files**\n- `ValidateOwnerFields.java` \u2014 the required-field check now tests the address in its *normalized* form, so a value that is blank only after trimming/collapsing is still rejected (400).\n- `Households.java` \u2014 split canonicalization into `normalizeName` (unchanged last-name handling) and `normalizeAddress` (delegates to `Addresses.normalize`), so both household duplicate detection (`matches`) and the shared household id (`id`) compare addresses using the normalization. This robustly handles pre-existing un-normalized data too, since both sides are normalized at comparison time.\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted the `normalizeAddress` step (governed by the same transaction) after `normalizeEmail` and before the uniqueness/build steps, so the household checks and `BuildOwner` all see the normalized address.\n- Fixed a now-dangling `@link` in `EnsureUniqueHousehold`'s javadoc.\n\nThe change is scoped to owner **creation** (POST) per the spec; the PUT/update path was left untouched.\n\nBehavior verified by the three acceptance tests: whitespace/case normalization (`\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`), abbreviation expansion (`\"7 elm ave\"` \u2192 `\"7 ELM AVENUE\"`), and blank-after-normalize rejection (`\"   \"` \u2192 400).",
